Today I’m diving into creating multiple containers on one server using Docker Compose. 

🔧 Essential Tools for This Setup

For this project, the key tools used are: 
- Docker 
- Docker Compose 
- tree (for visualizing directory structure)

These work in unison to get Docker Compose up and running smoothly on your server.

🌐 A Quick Note on Reverse Proxies
A reverse proxy sits in front of your containers to handle incoming web requests, directing them to the right service. This provides load balancing, improved security, and centralized logging. 

🐳 Docker Compose: Multi-Container Deployment with Replicas
Below is a sample Docker Compose file demonstrating my setup:

version: "3"
services:
 busticket:
 build: ./busticket/
 deploy:
 replicas: 2
 ports:
 - "80”

How It Works :

- Replicas: 
The line deploy.replicas: 2 instructs Docker to spin up two instances (replicas) of the same service on a single server. Each replica is an independent container instance, so if one fails, the other continues handling the load—ensuring higher availability.

- Port Mapping: 
By specifying ports: - "80", the container’s port 80 is exposed to the host. To check the exact port assignments and access details, you can use the following command:

docker-compose ps
 
This command lists the running containers along with the host port(s) that map to your container’s port 80. 🚀

📁 The Busticket Dockerfile & Folder Structure

Inside your busticket folder (which also contains your source code), create a Dockerfile with the content below:

dockerfile: 
FROM httpd
COPY . /usr/local/apache2/htdocs/

Place your source code alongside this Dockerfile in the busticket folder. To verify your directory structure on the server, simply run:

tree

🔑 Accessing Your Webapps & Checking Host Ports

Once you deploy with Docker Compose, your webapps become accessible via your server’s IP on the mapped port. To determine which port to use (especially if Docker has assigned one dynamically), run:

docker-compose ps

This command will list the container status along with the host port(s) that map to your container’s port 80. Simply open your browser and navigate to:


http://<YOUR_SERVER_IP>:<MAPPED_PORT>


Replace <YOUR_SERVER_IP> with your server’s IP address and <MAPPED_PORT> with the port shown in the command output.
